From fe247ad756e058bca34f041e1f00c622493adfc5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 13:56:37 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] DOS-PORT: patch 0230 -- work vector: deterministic counts of
 the work that drives 486 time

Why not just what: every rate claim on this port costs a real-hardware
round (about an hour), and DOSBox-X time is not evidence of 486 time
(PLAN.md: an 18% emulator figure was 100x on hardware). Counts of work
are different: the same fixture does the same work under the emulator and
on the 486, so a vector of counts priced with the measured bus and blit
constants can screen an optimisation idea before the hardware round. That
only holds if the vector covers the work that actually costs time and is
validated against hardware cells already on disk (LAB-METRIC.md).

What was already counted (bg_px, rb_sprites, black_fill_px, rb_clear_px,
presents_real, RenderCounter) is re-read, not re-counted. What was missing
and is added, each an integer increment or an add of a size in hand, never
a clock read:
- script VM instructions and engine API calls (ccInstance::Run): MMD's
  character select spends 24.6 of 36 s in script_vm on the 486 and nothing
  counted it. A local counter, flushed on exit and before every
  SCMD_CALLEXT, because a blocking call runs nested ticks inside the
  frame and QuitGame() leaves via exit() without unwinding it.
- bytes handed to SDL_UpdateWindowSurface(Rects), clipped to the window
  (the backend copies exactly those rects): the push is the phase at the
  bus ceiling; dirtypresent reports px only, periodically.
- present-time RAM->RAM copy bytes, 1:1 and scaled, and the 8-bit
  unchanged-frame compare and cache bytes (added on FramesEqualFast's exit
  paths, the loops untouched).
- composited sprite px by blit class (opaque, masked, alpha), clipped to
  the clip in force: masked costs 19.4 cycles/px against a 7.8 copy floor,
  and rb_sprite_px is the nominal area of all classes together.
- file bytes read through FileStream (defined in Common, read by the
  engine at snapshot time).

The vector is split the same way the time is. tick_rate_steady excludes
stall ticks' self time and everything outside [first tick entry, last
tick exit], so the vector is snapshotted where the tick scope already
reads the clock, nested ticks are subtracted exactly as
dos_tick_children_us is, a stall tick's self delta goes to an excluded
bucket, and the last snapshot is taken at every exit, nested included,
matching dos_tick_last_exit (AGS Quest's AutoPilot ends inside nested
ticks). Reported in RUNMANI.LOG as one `DOS-PORT work scope=steady` line,
its `scope=excluded` complement (steady + excluded = the whole run), and
`workpersec` rows sampled at the existing once-per-second point.

The script-instruction increment is compiled only into a LAB binary
(make AGS_WORK_SCRIPT_INS=1, which adds -DDOSAGS_WORK_SCRIPT_INS to the
flags the build fingerprint hashes): on a fixed-cycles DOSBox-X (bit-
identical repeats per arm) it made MMD's script_vm 4.6% slower and, the
tick being saturated, its render rate 18% lower (9.52 -> 7.76), against
+0.9% busy per render on BENCH8 anim12. Everything else is per call, per
sprite, per present or per tick and stays in every build; the summary
line says script_ins_counted=0/1 so a timed build's 0 is not misread.

Not counted: allocations (needs a malloc wrap across every library, a
build change rather than a counter).

